<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Bourbon Restoration</h1><p class="st-meta">1814 CE – 1830 CE · capital Paris</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The return of the Bourbon kings after Napoleon, a constitutional monarchy under Louis XVIII and Charles X that ended in the July Revolution of 1830.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> abdication in April 1814 the allies and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senat-conservateur">French Senate</a> restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a>, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a>. He granted the Charter of 1814, which kept a parliament and many revolutionary gains. Napoleon's return in the Hundred Days of 1815 interrupted the regime until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had a two-chamber parliament elected by a very small, wealthy electorate. Moderate ministers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armand-emmanuel-du-plessis-duc-de-richelieu">Duke of Richelieu</a> paid off war indemnities and ended the allied occupation in 1818, while ultra-royalists pressed for revenge after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-white-terror">White Terror</a>. Charles X, crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> in 1825, compensated émigrés and favoured the Church.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In July 1830 Charles X issued ordinances dissolving parliament and curbing the press. Three days of revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> drove him into exile, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis-Philippe of Orléans</a> became king, beginning the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/july-monarchy">July Monarchy</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> had been taken weeks earlier.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bourbon-restoration.jpg" alt="Bourbon Restoration" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https:/